/* ==============================================================
   03 — Relative moves: approach, slide and retract
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     MCS500 only (R1)
   TEACHES   MoveLinRelTrf, MoveLinRelWrf, MoveJointsRel
   NEEDS     Robot activated, no tool mounted
   WATCH     A pick pattern that works at any angle, unchanged
   TIME      ~35 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THE COMMANDS

       MoveLinRelTrf(x, y, z, γ)
       MoveLinRelWrf(x, y, z, γ)

   Same four arguments as MoveLin, but they are an OFFSET from
   where the robot already is, not an absolute destination. Both
   move in a straight line. The difference is which way the
   offset points:

     MoveLinRelTrf   relative to the TOOL frame — "slide 20 mm
                     along the part", whichever way the tool
                     happens to be turned
     MoveLinRelWrf   relative to the WORLD frame — "20 mm towards
                     the back of the tray", regardless of how the
                     tool is turned

   ON A SCARA, THE DIFFERENCE IS SIDEWAYS ONLY

   Every frame on the MCS500 has its z pointing up. So "down
   30 mm" is the same in both frames — always z = -30, always
   straight down, always into the work. (Note the minus: down is
   negative here, on every frame, every time.)

   Where the two frames differ is in the horizontal plane. The
   TRF turns with the tool — with γ. The WRF does not. So the
   question to ask is: does this sideways move belong to the
   PART, or to the CELL?

   WHY THIS IS MORE THAN A CONVENIENCE

   In basic/04 the approach pattern was written in absolute
   coordinates:

       MovePose(180, 0, 100, 0)     above the part
       MoveLin(180, 0, 60, 0)       down onto it
       MoveLin(180, 0, 100, 0)      back up

   Every one of those z values has to be recomputed if the pick
   point moves. With a hundred pick points, that is a hundred
   pairs of numbers that can disagree with each other.

   Written relatively, the pattern becomes:

       MovePose(<the point above the part>)
       MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, -30, 0)    down
       ... grip, slide, lift ...

   Now there is ONE pose per pick point, and the approach
   distance is stated once. Teach a new point and the approach
   comes along for free.

   And the slide is where the TRF earns its keep. A part lying at
   45 degrees has to be slid out of its nest at 45 degrees.
   Written in the tool frame, "slide 20 mm along x" is one line
   that is correct at every angle. Written in the world frame, it
   is trigonometry, recomputed for every part.

   TRF OR WRF?

   Use TRF when the motion is about the part: slide it out of its
   nest, push it against its own stop, back off along its own
   edge. The tool was turned to match the part, so the tool frame
   already knows which way the part points.

   Use WRF when the motion is about the cell: step to the next
   row of a tray, clear a wall that runs along the bench, move
   towards the conveyor. The cell does not turn with the tool.

   These are linear moves, so they obey SetCartLinVel and all the
   MoveLin caveats: they can be refused if the line leaves the
   reachable area, and they hold the arm configuration.

   THE JOINT-SPACE COUSIN

       MoveJointsRel(Δθ1, Δθ2, Δd3, Δθ4)

   The same idea for joints: move each joint by an amount, from
   wherever it is. No Cartesian check, no straight-line promise —
   just "this much more". On a SCARA two of these are very useful:
   MoveJointsRel(0, 0, -30, 0) drops the shaft 30 mm, which is
   straight down by construction, and MoveJointsRel(0, 0, 0, 90)
   gives the shaft a quarter turn, whatever it was holding.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode, MoveJump
   settings — so this program starts from firmware defaults no
   matter what ran before it. Reactivating brings the motors
   back.

   This does NOT move the arm: the MCS500 has absolute encoders
   and never needs homing, which is why there is no Home() here.

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision, P-Stop 2 and calibration config
   all survive. Run 00_common/00_cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


SetJointVel(20)
SetCartLinVel(60)

MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(1)


/* --- The pick pattern, written once ----------------------------
   Below, the same four-line pattern runs at three different pick
   points, with the part lying at three different angles. The
   pattern itself never changes — only the MovePose line that
   puts the tool above the part, turned to match it. */

// --- Pick point 1: part lying square --------------------------
MovePose(170, -60, 80, 0)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, -30, 0)           // down onto the part
Delay(0.5)                            // (grip here)
MoveLinRelTrf(20, 0, 0, 0)            // slide 20 mm along the part
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, 30, 0)            // lift clear

Delay(1)

// --- Pick point 2: part lying at 45 degrees -------------------
// This is the one that proves the point. The part is turned,
// the tool is turned to match, and the slide line is unchanged —
// it still runs along the part, because "x" follows the tool. In
// absolute coordinates this slide would need a sine and a cosine.
MovePose(180, 0, 80, 45)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, -30, 0)
Delay(0.5)
MoveLinRelTrf(20, 0, 0, 0)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, 30, 0)

Delay(1)

// --- Pick point 3: part lying at -30 degrees ------------------
MovePose(170, 60, 80, -30)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, -30, 0)
Delay(0.5)
MoveLinRelTrf(20, 0, 0, 0)
MoveLinRelTrf(0, 0, 30, 0)

Delay(1)


/* --- TRF versus WRF, side by side -------------------------------
   Same turned pose, same 30 mm, two different directions. */

MovePose(170, 0, 80, 45)
Delay(0.5)

// Along the tool's x — the diagonal, at 45 degrees, the way the
// tool is turned.
MoveLinRelTrf(30, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(0.5)
MoveLinRelTrf(-30, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(0.5)

// Along the world's x — straight out from the base, regardless
// of the 45 degree turn. Watch the two moves back to back and
// the difference is unmistakable.
MoveLinRelWrf(30, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(0.5)
MoveLinRelWrf(-30, 0, 0, 0)
Delay(1)


/* --- The joint-space cousin ------------------------------------
   Same place, joint moves this time. Drop the shaft 30 mm, give
   it a quarter turn, undo both. Four lines that work from any
   starting position, because none of them names one. */
MoveJointsRel(0, 0, -30, 0)
Delay(0.5)
MoveJointsRel(0, 0, 0, 90)
Delay(0.5)
MoveJointsRel(0, 0, 0, -90)
MoveJointsRel(0, 0, 30, 0)


// --- Home -----------------------------------------------------
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Change every "30" in the pick patterns to "20" with a find
      and replace, and run again. The approach distance for the
      entire program just changed, in one edit. Compare that to
      editing absolute z values for every point.

   2. The 45 degree pick is the strongest single demonstration
      here. Show it, then ask how their current system handles
      parts that arrive at an angle — from a vision system, say.

   NEXT   04_posture_configuration.mxprog — the two different
          ways to reach one pose.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
